{
  "assessment": "The source supports a matched pair of normalized Lyapunov scaling laws over the DNS Reynolds-number range Re in [50,1400]. The DNS exponent alpha=0.59 is explicitly reported with uncertainty, and the Kolmogorov mean-field prediction lambda ~ sqrt(Re) (alpha=0.5) is explicitly presented as a competing law on the same normalized domain. Both branches are encoded as (Re/50)^alpha using anchored parameters. The source does not report an amplitude for either law, so the normalized form is used without an unreported prefactor. The source supports the DNS range and the two exponents, so case_authoring_ready is true.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Aikya Banerjee, Intermittent Fluctuations Determine the Nature of Chaos in Turbulence, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/1dj2-zw28",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/1dj2-zw28",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2505.09538v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper studies the Reynolds-number dependence of the largest Lyapunov exponent in fully developed turbulence using decorrelators, DNS of the Navier-Stokes equation, and the GOY shell model. It reports a universal scaling lambda ~ Re^alpha with alpha=0.59±0.04, departing from the Kolmogorov mean-field prediction lambda ~ sqrt(Re). The selected cluster focuses on the DNS-based normalized Lyapunov scaling over Re in [50,1400].",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "lyapunov_scaling_dns",
      "statement": "The largest Lyapunov exponent from DNS follows a power-law scaling with Reynolds number: lambda_DNS ~ Re^alpha, with alpha=0.59±0.04. This is the central empirical law extracted from the exponential-growth phase of the decorrelator.",
      "equations": [
        "lambda_DNS ~ Re^alpha, alpha=0.59±0.04"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "lambda_DNS",
        "Re",
        "alpha"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "alpha_dns",
          "value": 0.59,
          "uncertainty": "±0.04 (convention source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "scaling exponent for lambda_DNS vs Re",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 388,
              "line_end": 389,
              "support": "All Lyapunov exponents follow a universal scaling lambda_DNS = lambda_GOY = lambda ~ Re^alpha, with alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 46,
              "line_end": 47,
              "support": "We obtain a precise value of alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Re_ref_50",
          "value": 50.0,
          "uncertainty": "None reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "reference Reynolds number for normalization",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 467,
              "line_end": 467,
              "support": "Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400, so Re=50 is the lower bound of the DNS range."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 388,
          "line_end": 389,
          "support": "All Lyapunov exponents follow a universal scaling lambda_DNS = lambda_GOY = lambda ~ Re^alpha, with alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 46,
          "line_end": 47,
          "support": "We obtain a precise value of alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "kolmogorov_mean_field_scaling",
      "statement": "The Kolmogorov mean-field prediction for the Lyapunov exponent is lambda ~ sqrt(Re), i.e., alpha=0.5. This is presented as the competing law that the measured DNS exponent departs from.",
      "equations": [
        "lambda ~ sqrt(Re) = Re^0.5"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "lambda",
        "Re"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "alpha_kolmogorov",
          "value": 0.5,
          "uncertainty": "None reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "scaling exponent for lambda vs Re in the Kolmogorov mean-field prediction",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 78,
              "line_end": 78,
              "support": "Ruelle showed that lambda ∝ sqrt(Re)."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 395,
              "line_end": 395,
              "support": "The departure of the measured Lyapunov exponents from a Kolmogorov-like mean field result lambda ~ <gamma_3> ~ sqrt(Re)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Re_ref_50_kolmogorov",
          "value": 50.0,
          "uncertainty": "None reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "reference Reynolds number for normalization in the Kolmogorov branch",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 467,
              "line_end": 467,
              "support": "Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400, so Re=50 is the lower bound of the DNS range."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "Ruelle showed that lambda ∝ sqrt(Re)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 395,
          "line_end": 395,
          "support": "The departure of the measured Lyapunov exponents from a Kolmogorov-like mean field result lambda ~ <gamma_3> ~ sqrt(Re)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "dns_domain_2pi",
      "statement": "DNS on a 2π triply-periodic domain with pseudospectral method, constant energy injection, and Reynolds numbers in [50,1400].",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 277,
          "line_end": 278,
          "support": "Solving the incompressible Navier-Stokes equation on a 2π triply-periodic domain by using a pseudospectral method."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 467,
          "line_end": 467,
          "support": "Vary the coefficient viscosity 10^-3 ≤ ν ≤ 32×10^-3 to obtain Reynolds numbers 50 ≤ Re ≤ 1400."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "dns_perturbation",
      "statement": "Gaussian perturbation with epsilon_0=10^-5, sigma_0=4 dx, localized at domain center, used to initialize system B.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 480,
          "line_end": 481,
          "support": "We use a Gaussian perturbation δu_0(x) = epsilon_0 exp(-(x-x_0)^2/2 sigma_0^2) x-hat with epsilon_0=10^-5, sigma_0=4 dx, x_0=(π,π,π)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "intermittent_fluctuations_included",
      "statement": "The measured departure from the Kolmogorov mean-field result is attributed to intermittent fluctuations in the velocity-gradient tensor, captured by gamma_3^std ~ Re^0.59.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 46,
          "line_end": 48,
          "support": "We obtain a precise value of alpha = 0.59 ± 0.04 and show that departure from the Kolmogorov mean field result lambda ∝ sqrt(Re) is a consequence of the intermittent fluctuations in the velocity-gradient tensor."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 393,
          "line_end": 400,
          "support": "The departure of the measured Lyapunov exponents from a Kolmogorov-like mean field result lambda ~ <gamma_3> ~ sqrt(Re), is a clear indication that the deviation must stem from intermittent fluctuations and the dominant scaling in the statistics of gamma_3 is due to gamma_3^std ~ Re^alpha."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "monofractal_kolmogorov_mean_field",
      "statement": "The Kolmogorov mean-field prediction assumes a monofractal flow with a unique Hölder exponent h=1/3, leading to lambda ~ sqrt(Re).",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 75,
          "line_end": 84,
          "support": "Ruelle showed that lambda ∝ sqrt(Re). This result is a consequence of associating the (largest) Lyapunov exponent with the inverse of the smallest time-scale of the flow tau_eta ∝ 1/sqrt(Re). More generally, assuming a Hölder exponent h characterising the flow, it is easy to show lambda ∝ Re^{(1-h)/(1+h)}; indeed taking the monofractal, Kolmogorov limit of a unique h = 1/3 recovers the Ruelle scaling."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
